      <span class="meta-val">Windows x64</span>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <span class="meta-label">Samples</span>
      <span class="meta-val">1</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="hero-divider"></div>
</div>

<!-- MAIN -->
<div class="layout">

  <!-- SIDEBAR -->
  <aside class="sidebar">
    <div class="sidebar-section">
      <div class="sidebar-label">Sections</div>
      <ul class="sidebar-nav">
        <li><a href="#sample">Sample &amp; Hashes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#analysis">Technical Analysis</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mitigation">Mitigation</a></li>
        <li><a href="#yara">YARA Rule</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="sidebar-section">
      <div class="sidebar-label">Rule Files</div>
      <ul class="sidebar-nav">
        <li><a href="#yara-settra">settra_blinder.yar</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </aside>

  <!-- CONTENT -->
  <main class="content">

    <!-- SAMPLE -->
    <section id="sample">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">01</span>
        <h2>Sample &amp; Hashes</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Settra Blinder is a 64-bit Windows PE command-line tool designed to disable Endpoint Detection and Response protections via kernel-level attack techniques.</p>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Property</th><th>Value</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td class="label">Filename</td><td class="val">edr_blind.exe</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="label">Filetype</td><td class="val">PE32+ executable (console) x86-64 (stripped to external PDB), for MS Windows</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="label">Size</td><td class="val">79,360 bytes</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="label">MD5</td><td class="hash">d5e6b89d4ae598ad889a46d352ce722f</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="label">SHA1</td><td class="hash">bdc63e59aa9e9317ba4f40222748be1729a810b2</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="label">SHA256</td><td class="hash">32d6657506d861c206a5c47c62bba4c82231d4a12f12d258908a46f7e5f637bc</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="label">YARA Rule</td><td><span class="tag tag-all">settra_blinder</span></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- ANALYSIS -->
    <section id="analysis">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">02</span>
        <h2>Technical Analysis</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Settra Blinder demonstrates sophisticated kernel-level attack capabilities across several technique categories:</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <strong>BYOVD — Bring Your Own Vulnerable Driver:</strong> Exploits legitimately signed but vulnerable drivers to gain kernel read/write primitives. Known targeted drivers include RTCore64.sys (<span style="color:#a5d6ff;font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:12px">CVE-2019-18093</span>) and AsrDrv101.sys (<span style="color:#a5d6ff;font-family:var(--font-mono);font-size:12px">CVE-2020-12446</span>).
      </div>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Technique</th><th>Description</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">BYOVD Exploitation</td>
              <td class="val">Loads vulnerable signed drivers (RTCore64.sys, AsrDrv101.sys) to gain kernel memory access without triggering DSE</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">Direct Syscall Invocation</td>
              <td class="val">Bypasses user-mode API hooks by invoking syscalls directly, evading EDR instrumentation at the API layer</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">PPL Bypass</td>
              <td class="val">Circumvents Process Protection Level controls to access and manipulate protected security processes</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">Ps Callback Enumeration</td>
              <td class="val">Enumerates and disables PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine, PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine, and PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutineEx kernel callbacks used by EDR sensors</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">ETW-TI Patching</td>
              <td class="val">Patches Event Tracing for Windows Threat Intelligence providers via direct memory manipulation, blinding kernel telemetry collection</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">AMSI Patching</td>
              <td class="val">Disables the Antimalware Scan Interface through in-memory patching, preventing script and content scanning</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="label">PPID Spoofing</td>
              <td class="val">Spoofs process parent IDs to simulate creation from legitimate system processes such as explorer.exe</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- MITIGATION -->
    <section id="mitigation">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">03</span>
        <h2>Mitigation</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Accenture Cyber Intelligence recommends the following measures to mitigate the risk posed by Settra Blinder:</p>
      <ul class="mitigation-list">
        <li>Implement and maintain updated kernel patches to prevent exploitation of known vulnerable drivers.</li>
        <li>Deploy Driver Signature Enforcement (DSE) and Secure Boot to prevent unsigned or vulnerable driver loading.</li>
        <li>Implement strict access controls to prevent unauthorized access to kernel interfaces and system management functions.</li>
        <li>Keep operating system and kernel patches updated to mitigate vulnerabilities that could be exploited via BYOVD techniques.</li>
        <li>Enable system logging including kernel-level events, driver activities, and security configuration changes to support detection and forensic investigation.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- YARA -->
    <section id="yara">
      <div class="section-header">
        <span class="section-number">04</span>
        <h2>YARA Rule</h2>
      </div>
      <p>The following YARA rule detects Settra Blinder based on CLI argument strings, vulnerable driver device paths, kernel callback routine names, and runtime log strings. Security teams are encouraged to deploy this rule across endpoint and file-scanning solutions.</p>

      <div id="yara-settra" class="yara-block">
        <div class="yara-header">
          <span class="yara-name">settra_blinder.yar</span>
          <div class="yara-meta-tags">
            <span class="yara-meta-tag">Windows x64</span>
            <span class="yara-meta-tag">10 Jul 2026</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <pre><code><span class="kw">rule</span> <span class="fn">settra_blinder</span>
{
  <span class="kw">meta:</span>
    <span class="nm">description</span> = <span class="st">"Detects EDR Blinder"</span>
    <span class="nm">author</span>      = <span class="st">"Accenture Cyber Intelligence"</span>
    <span class="nm">date</span>        = <span class="st">"2026-07-10"</span>
    <span class="nm">hash</span>        = <span class="st">"32d6657506d861c206a5c47c62bba4c82231d4a12f12d258908a46f7e5f637bc"</span>

  <span class="kw">strings:</span>
    <span class="cm">// CLI argument strings</span>
    <span class="nm">$args1</span> = <span class="st">"--asrdrv"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$args2</span> = <span class="st">"--ppid"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$args3</span> = <span class="st">"--kill-loop"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$args4</span> = <span class="st">"--stpm"</span> ascii wide

    <span class="cm">// Vulnerable driver device paths (BYOVD targets)</span>
    <span class="nm">$drvdev1</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\RTCore64"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$drvdev2</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\AsrDrv101"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$drvdev3</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\gdrv"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$drvdev4</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\HpPortIox64"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$drvdev5</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\PhysMem"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$drvdev6</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\WinRing0_1_2_0"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$drvdev7</span> = <span class="st">"\\\\.\\STProcessMonitorDriver"</span> ascii wide

    <span class="cm">// Kernel callback routine names</span>
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct1</span> = <span class="st">"PsSetCreateProcessNotifyRoutine"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct2</span> = <span class="st">"PsSetCreateThreadNotifyRoutine"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct3</span> = <span class="st">"PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutineEx"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct4</span> = <span class="st">"PsSetLoadImageNotifyRoutine"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct5</span> = <span class="st">"PsCreateProcess"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct6</span> = <span class="st">"PsCreateThread"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct7</span> = <span class="st">"PsLoadImage"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct8</span> = <span class="st">"ObProcess"</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$krlstruct9</span> = <span class="st">"ObThread"</span> ascii wide

    <span class="cm">// Runtime log strings</span>
    <span class="nm">$log1</span> = <span class="st">"[*] === ETW Threat Intelligence Provider Bypass ==="</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$log2</span> = <span class="st">"[*] === PPL Bypass ==="</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$log3</span> = <span class="st">"[*] === ETW + AMSI self-patch ==="</span> ascii wide
    <span class="nm">$log4</span> = <span class="st">"[*] === Minifilter Scan ==="</span> ascii wide

  <span class="kw">condition:</span>
    3 of (<span class="nm">$args*</span>) and 4 of (<span class="nm">$drvdev*</span>) and 5 of (<span class="nm">$krlstruct*</span>) and 2 of (<span class="nm">$log*</span>)
}</code></pre>
      </div>

      <p style="margin-top:1rem;font-size:13px;">The condition requires a minimum threshold across all four string groups — reducing false positives while tolerating minor sample variation. Any binary matching 3+ CLI args, 4+ driver paths, 5+ kernel callback names, and 2+ log strings is a high-confidence match.</p>
    </section>

  </main>
</div>

<footer>
  <span>Accenture Apex Labs</span> · Threat Intelligence · TLP:CLEAR · July 2026
</footer>

<script>
  const sections = document.querySelectorAll('section[id]');
  const links = document.querySelectorAll('.sidebar-nav a');

  const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
    entries.forEach(e => {
      if (e.isIntersecting) {
        links.forEach(l => l.classList.remove('active'));
        const active = document.querySelector(`.sidebar-nav a[href="#${e.target.id}"]`);
        if (active) active.classList.add('active');
      }
    });
  }, { rootMargin: '-20% 0px -70% 0px' });

  sections.forEach(s => observer.observe(s));
</script>
</body>
</html>
